#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# find_max_value

#% text
# `find_max_value(f, [x, x0])` gives the value of a local maximum of `f` found by a local search from `x = x0`, with the arguments and options of `find_maximum`.

#% text
# `find_max_value([f, cons...], vars)` subject to constraints.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# It is `find_maximum` with only the value or the position kept; the position is always a list of the variables' values.

#% item
# Without a starting value the search starts at 1.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% text
# A local extremum near a start:

#% cell
find_max_value(x*cos(x), [x, 5])
#% output
#= 6.361003944833854

#% text
# Several variables:

#% cell
find_max_value(sin(x)*sin(2y), [[x, 2], [y, 2]])
#% output
#= 1.0

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# With a constraint:

#% cell
find_max_value([x - 2y, x^2 + y^2 <= 1], [x, y])
#% output
#= 2.23606797749979

#% text
# A linear program:

#% cell
find_max_value([x + y, 3x + 2y >= 7 and x >= 0 and y >= 0], [x, y])
#% output
#| find_maximum: the objective is unbounded on the feasible set
#= inf

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `find_maximum`, `find_minimum`, `find_maximum`, `n_minimize`
